\title{
    \vspace{-2em}
    \textbf{\Large AXON-RFC-006 Appendix B (v0.5):}\\[0.2em]
    \textbf{\Large \texttt{easynet pages} --- publishing a folder as a website}\\[0.4em]
    \large Ability-on-resource design, kernel-enforced read sandbox,\\
    \large minimum deliverable: \texttt{hello-world.html + style.css}
}
\author{
    Silan Hu \\ \small National University of Singapore
}
\date{Draft v0.5, \today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Publishing a folder as a website is a single ability invocation
on EasyNet. The act mints one resource URA. The abilities that
read the published bytes are owned by that resource, not by the
publishing agent. The Hub serves bytes by invoking those
resource-owned abilities through the regular dispatcher and
streaming the result over HTTP. There is no \texttt{web\_app}
type, no state machine, no parallel ontology, and no new wire
verb. v0.5 specifies the minimum deliverable that proves the
shape end-to-end --- two files, \texttt{hello-world.html} and
\texttt{style.css}, served over a real browser fetch through a
kernel-enforced read sandbox --- and frames everything beyond it
(SPA, API surface, peer-agent action discovery, federation,
SCOPED visibility) as post-MVP work that builds on this same
ability-on-resource shape without changing it.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents
\vspace{1em}
\hrule
\vspace{1em}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{What changed from v0.4}
\label{sec:v04-diff}

v0.4 placed the lifecycle of a published web app inside a
\texttt{Resource} ent row and routed all reads through an ability
hosted on the publishing agent
(\texttt{<owner>.web\_app.fetch}). Three CTO clarifications
collapsed both choices:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X}
\toprule
\textbf{v0.4 had} & \textbf{v0.5 has} \\
\midrule
A new \texttt{web\_app} concept (state machine, ent fields, abilities, surface)
 & No new concept. Publishing is one ability call. The thing produced is a single resource URA. \\
Read ability rooted at the publishing agent
(\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.fetch})
 & Read ability rooted at the resource itself
(\texttt{ability/pages.<rid>.fetch}). Namespace under \texttt{ability/} is the resource id, not the agent id. \\
URL prefix invented (\texttt{/web/<owner>/<slug>/...})
 & URL form is fixed: \texttt{<rid>.pages.<realm>/<path-under-folder>}. \\
A bespoke five-state lifecycle (Created/Building/Built/Failed/Deleted)
 & No lifecycle enum. The published-or-not state lives in the daemon's in-memory \texttt{PublishedResources} map and the receipt chain. \\
Phase 1 deliverable was a Vite+React shop with API and \texttt{actions.*}
 & Phase 1 is a single folder containing \texttt{hello-world.html} + \texttt{style.css}. Everything else is post-MVP. \\
Read path was a user-space path-traversal check
 & Read path is a kernel-enforced sandbox (\texttt{openat2} with \texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH} on Linux; equivalent on macOS). \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\noindent The result is a much smaller specification. The
deliverable shrinks from a ``full agent-deployed full-stack
app'' to ``a folder of static bytes accessible over HTTPS
through a sandboxed read path,'' which is in fact the only
shape any of the larger goals could be built on top of.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{The single primitive}
\label{sec:primitive}

\subsection{Publishing a folder is one ability invocation}

\normsv{} The publishing agent invokes one ability on itself:

\begin{lstlisting}
caller   = caller_agent_uri    // typically the publishing agent
callee   = caller_agent_uri    // self-call
ability  = ability/<caller_agent_uri>.pages.publish
subject  = user/<caller_username>           // the publisher's user URA
                                            // (the folder doesn't exist as a
                                            //  resource yet — it is minted by
                                            //  this invocation)
args     = {
    folder:     "/Users/alice/site",        // absolute local path
    visibility: "PUBLIC"                    // PUBLIC | PRIVATE | SCOPED
                                            //   (v0.5 implements PUBLIC only;
                                            //    PRIVATE/SCOPED reserved.)
}
\end{lstlisting}

The successful receipt of this invocation states, in
\texttt{state\_transition.post\_state}, the resource URA that was
minted:

\begin{lstlisting}
resource URA:   easynet:///r/<realm>/resource/pages.<rid>
\end{lstlisting}

\texttt{<rid>} is a content-addressed identifier (sha256 of the
canonicalised folder Merkle, truncated to 16 hex chars in v0.5)
and is therefore stable across daemon restarts and across hosts
holding the same bytes.

\subsection{The abilities that read the bytes are owned by the resource}

\normsv{} \textbf{This is the centre of the design.}
The abilities through which a caller reads the published bytes
do \emph{not} live under the publishing agent's namespace. They
live under the resource's namespace:

\begin{lstlisting}
ability URA:   easynet:///r/<realm>/ability/pages.<rid>.fetch
                                            └── resource id ──┘└name┘
\end{lstlisting}

When publish completes, the daemon registers exactly one ability
in its registry under that name. The ability's executor is the
daemon (substrate); the namespace owner is the resource
(\texttt{pages.<rid>}); the agent (\texttt{alice}) is neither
the namespace owner nor the executor name --- it is the
\emph{host} of the substrate, recorded as the callee in
invocation envelopes (per URA v4.1.5 §9, callee is constrained
to \texttt{hub | device | agent}).

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-1 (ability ownership)]
For a resource published via \texttt{pages.publish}, every
ability that operates on that resource's bytes \textbf{MUST} be
registered under the namespace \texttt{ability/pages.<rid>.*}.
Registering the same ability under the publishing agent's
namespace (\texttt{ability/<owner>.<name>}) is non-conformant.
The ontological reason: this fetch ability has no meaning
outside the specific resource that minted it; rooting it at the
agent would attach it to a scope larger than its lifetime.
\end{invariant}

\begin{notebox}
\textbf{Why this matches v4.1.5 callee constraints.} URA v4.1.5
§9 fixes \texttt{callee \(\in\) \{hub, device, agent\}}: a wire
addressee must be a process that holds a keypair and signs
envelopes. Resources do not. The callee on a fetch invocation is
therefore the host agent (\texttt{agent/<host>}), and only the
\texttt{ability} field carries the resource scoping. The
ontology shift is in ability-namespace ownership, not in callee
typing.
\end{notebox}

\subsection{The fetch invocation, end-to-end}

\normsv{} A browser GET on the published URL is rendered into:

\begin{lstlisting}
caller    = hub/01HUB                                      // see §3.1
callee    = agent/<host of pages.<rid>>                    // alice in this RFC
ability   = ability/pages.<rid>.fetch
subject   = resource/pages.<rid>
args      = { path: "/hello-world.html" }
nonce     = <128-bit>
causal_context = null                                      // root for this fetch
\end{lstlisting}

The receipt for this invocation is \emph{operational} (lossy,
observability), not canonical, because the call is read-only and
state-preserving. See §\ref{sec:receipts} for the two-tier
receipt classification.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Hub: the only HTTP boundary}
\label{sec:hub}

\subsection{Hub is an agent that owns one ability}

\normsv{} The Hub binds the wildcard host pattern
\texttt{*.pages.<realm>} (in production:
\texttt{*.pages.easynet.run}) on its TLS listener. On any HTTP
request matching this pattern, the Hub invokes its own built-in
ability:

\begin{lstlisting}
caller   = hub/01HUB                              // self-as-caller
callee   = hub/01HUB                              // self-call
ability  = ability/01HUB.pages.serve
subject  = resource/pages.<rid>                   // parsed from subdomain
args     = {
    path:        "/hello-world.html",             // from HTTP path
    method:      "GET",
    request_id:  "...",
    user_agent:  "..."
}
\end{lstlisting}

The Hub's \texttt{pages.serve} ability:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item Parses the subdomain \(\to\) \texttt{<rid>}.
    \item Locates the host agent of that resource through the
    federation directory (in MVP: a single host, the same
    daemon that performed the publish).
    \item Issues a \texttt{forward\_invoke} to
    \texttt{ability/pages.<rid>.fetch} on that host.
    \item Streams the returned bytes back to the browser with
    the Content-Type the fetch ability returned.
    \item Emits one operational receipt for the serve.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-2 (one Hub boundary)]
HTTP requests for \texttt{*.pages.<realm>} \textbf{MUST} enter
EasyNet through the \texttt{ability/01HUB.pages.serve} call site
and \textbf{MUST NOT} bypass it. The Hub is the sole translator
between the HTTP world and the axon-bus world; there is no
private back-channel that lets static bytes flow without the
\texttt{forward\_invoke} round trip.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{URL form}
\label{sec:url}

\normsv{}

\begin{lstlisting}
https://<rid>.pages.<realm>/<path-under-published-folder>
\end{lstlisting}

For the production realm:

\begin{lstlisting}
https://9c8b3a4f7e2dabcd.pages.easynet.run/hello-world.html
https://9c8b3a4f7e2dabcd.pages.easynet.run/style.css
\end{lstlisting}

The subdomain holds the resource id; the path holds the
file path under the published folder, verbatim. There is no
implicit owner segment, no slug segment, no ontological prefix.
URL is HTTP transport; URA is axon identity; the Hub is the only
component that maps between them.

\begin{notebox}
\textbf{rid length.} v0.5 uses a 16-hex-char (64-bit) prefix of
the folder's content hash. This is sufficient for PUBLIC
visibility (collision probability negligible at expected
publish counts) and is short enough to type. PRIVATE/SCOPED
publish, when implemented, must use the full 64-hex-char hash
or a separately-minted unguessable token, since the URL of a
PRIVATE publish must function as a capability and an 8-byte
prefix is enumerable.
\end{notebox}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Daemon: the read sandbox}
\label{sec:sandbox}

\normsv{} \texttt{ability/pages.<rid>.fetch} \textbf{MUST} run
inside a kernel-enforced read sandbox. User-space path
validation alone is non-conformant.

\subsection{Mechanism (Linux)}

\normsv{} The daemon, on \texttt{pages.publish}, opens the
folder as a directory file descriptor:

\begin{lstlisting}
let folder_fd = open(folder_path, O_DIRECTORY | O_CLOEXEC)?;
PublishedResources.insert(rid, PublishHandle {
    folder_fd,                                    // kept for the lifetime
    visibility,                                   // of the publish
    canonical_root: canonicalize(folder_path)?,
    file_size_cap: 100 * 1024 * 1024,            // 100 MiB per file
});
\end{lstlisting}

On \texttt{fetch(path)}:

\begin{lstlisting}
let file_fd = openat2(
    handle.folder_fd,
    args.path.trim_start_matches('/'),
    OpenHow {
        flags:    O_RDONLY | O_NOFOLLOW | O_CLOEXEC,
        mode:     0,
        resolve:  RESOLVE_BENEATH                  // kernel guarantees:
                  | RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS            //   path may not escape
                  | RESOLVE_NO_MAGICLINKS,         //   handle.folder_fd
    },
)?;
let metadata = fstat(file_fd)?;
require!(metadata.is_regular_file());              // deny FIFO/dev/socket
require!(metadata.size <= handle.file_size_cap);
let bytes = read_to_end(file_fd)?;
let mime  = mime_from_extension(args.path);        // allow-list table
return FetchResponse { bytes, content_type: mime };
\end{lstlisting}

\texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH} is the kernel's contract that path
resolution never crosses out of \texttt{folder\_fd}, no matter
how many \texttt{..} components the input contains, no matter
what symlinks point where, no matter how the filesystem
mutates between calls.

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-3 (kernel-enforced sandbox)]
Linux daemons \textbf{MUST} use \texttt{openat2(2)} with
\texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH | RESOLVE\_NO\_SYMLINKS |
RESOLVE\_NO\_MAGICLINKS} for every fetch read. The fall-back to
\texttt{realpath()}-then-prefix-check is non-conformant: it
races the filesystem and is bypassable through TOCTOU.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{Mechanism (macOS)}

\normsv{} macOS lacks \texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH}. v0.5 mitigates
through a combination:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item Resolve the path with \texttt{realpath(3)}; require
    \texttt{starts\_with(handle.canonical\_root)}.
    \item Open with \texttt{O\_NOFOLLOW} + \texttt{O\_CLOEXEC}.
    \item Wrap the daemon process in a Mach
    \texttt{sandbox\_init}/\texttt{sandbox-exec} profile
    declaring read-only access to the published folders only.
\end{enumerate}

The macOS combination is structurally weaker than
\texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH}; v0.5 ships it because the realistic
production target is Linux. Strengthening macOS to a per-publish
sandboxed worker is deferred (see §\ref{sec:open}).

\subsection{Default-deny content discipline}

\normsv{} Independent of the kernel sandbox, fetch enforces:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item Files whose name begins with \texttt{.} (dotfiles) are
    refused with 404. \texttt{.git/}, \texttt{.env},
    \texttt{.DS\_Store}, etc.\ are never served.
    \item Extensions outside the MIME allow-list are served as
    \texttt{application/octet-stream} with a
    \texttt{Content-Disposition: attachment} header so the
    browser cannot interpret them.
    \item Per-file size cap (default 100 MiB; configurable).
    Files exceeding the cap return 502 to the Hub.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Allow-list MIME table}

\normsv{} v0.5's MIME allow-list:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{tabular}{l l}
\toprule
\textbf{Extension} & \textbf{Content-Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{.html}        & \texttt{text/html; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.htm}         & \texttt{text/html; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.css}         & \texttt{text/css; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.js, .mjs}    & \texttt{application/javascript; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.json}        & \texttt{application/json; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.svg}         & \texttt{image/svg+xml} \\
\texttt{.png}         & \texttt{image/png} \\
\texttt{.jpg, .jpeg}  & \texttt{image/jpeg} \\
\texttt{.gif}         & \texttt{image/gif} \\
\texttt{.webp}        & \texttt{image/webp} \\
\texttt{.woff, .woff2}& \texttt{font/woff[2]} \\
\texttt{.txt, .md}    & \texttt{text/plain; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{(other)}      & \texttt{application/octet-stream} (download) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Receipts: two tiers}
\label{sec:receipts}

\normsv{} v0.5 adopts the two-tier classification stated in
the AXIOM (every invocation produces a receipt) but
distinguishes:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X X}
\toprule
\textbf{Tier} & \textbf{Used for} & \textbf{Storage} \\
\midrule
Canonical
 & \texttt{pages.publish} (state-changing). One receipt per call. Persistent. Walk-chained.
 & The receipt-store landing in URA v2 plan §Phase 6. \\
Operational
 & \texttt{pages.<rid>.fetch}, \texttt{01HUB.pages.serve} (state-preserving, idempotent reads). One receipt per call.
 & Bounded ring buffer (lossy under load). Aggregated rates published to \texttt{observe.health}. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-4 (idempotent reads are operational)]
\texttt{pages.<rid>.fetch} and \texttt{01HUB.pages.serve}
\textbf{MUST} be classified as operational receipts. Forcing
read-path receipts onto the canonical chain saturates the chain
under any realistic browser load (a single SPA page yields tens
of fetch calls per visit) and erases the signal-to-noise
property the canonical chain depends on.
\end{invariant}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Minimum deliverable: \texttt{hello-world.html + style.css}}
\label{sec:mvp}

\subsection{Scenario}

The publishing folder:

\begin{lstlisting}
/Users/alice/site/
    hello-world.html
    style.css
\end{lstlisting}

Contents:

\begin{lstlisting}
hello-world.html
————————————————————————————————————————————————
<!doctype html>
<html>
  <head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
    <title>Hello</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Hello, EasyNet.</h1>
  </body>
</html>

style.css
————————————————————————————————————————————————
h1 { color: red; font-family: ui-sans-serif; }
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{End-to-end trace}

\textbf{Publish}:

\begin{lstlisting}
$ easynet ability invoke <alice>.pages.publish \
    --args '{"folder":"/Users/alice/site","visibility":"PUBLIC"}'

→ {
    "rid": "9c8b3a4f7e2dabcd",
    "url": "https://9c8b3a4f7e2dabcd.pages.easynet.run/"
  }
\end{lstlisting}

Daemon side:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item Computes folder Merkle hash; takes 16-hex prefix.
    \item \texttt{open("/Users/alice/site", O\_DIRECTORY)}; keeps fd.
    \item Inserts \texttt{(rid \(\to\) handle)} into in-memory \texttt{PublishedResources}.
    \item Registers \texttt{ability/pages.<rid>.fetch} into the local ability registry.
    \item Emits canonical receipt: \texttt{ability=<alice>.pages.publish}, \texttt{subject=user/alice}, \texttt{post=resource/pages.<rid>}.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Browser fetch (HTML)}:

\begin{lstlisting}
GET /hello-world.html HTTP/1.1
Host: 9c8b3a4f7e2dabcd.pages.easynet.run
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Hub:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item Subdomain \(\to\) \texttt{rid = 9c8b3a4f7e2dabcd}.
    \item Directory lookup \(\to\) host agent \texttt{<alice>}.
    \item \texttt{forward\_invoke}: \\
    \quad \texttt{caller = hub/01HUB,
                  callee = agent/<alice>,
                  ability = ability/pages.<rid>.fetch,
                  subject = resource/pages.<rid>,
                  args = \{path:"/hello-world.html"\}}.
    \item Operational receipt for the serve.
\end{itemize}

\noindent Daemon:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item \texttt{openat2(folder\_fd, "hello-world.html", RESOLVE\_BENEATH \dots)}.
    \item \texttt{fstat} \(\to\) regular file, 173 bytes.
    \item Read; MIME = \texttt{text/html; charset=utf-8}.
    \item Operational receipt for the fetch.
    \item Returns \texttt{FetchResponse}.
\end{itemize}

\noindent Browser response:

\begin{lstlisting}
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
Cache-Control: max-age=60
Content-Length: 173

<!doctype html>
<html>
  <head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
    ...
\end{lstlisting}

\textbf{Browser fetch (CSS)}: identical pattern,
\texttt{path = "/style.css"}, MIME \texttt{text/css}. Browser
parses CSS, applies \texttt{color: red} to the \texttt{h1}.

\textbf{Path-traversal attempt}:

\begin{lstlisting}
GET /../../etc/passwd HTTP/1.1
Host: 9c8b3a4f7e2dabcd.pages.easynet.run

→ HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Daemon's \texttt{openat2} returns \texttt{EXDEV} (or
\texttt{ELOOP}, depending on kernel version) because path
resolution attempted to escape \texttt{folder\_fd}. The kernel
itself refuses the open; the daemon never reads
\texttt{/etc/passwd}; the Hub renders 404.

\subsection{Test matrix}

\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{xltabular}{\linewidth}{l l L{3.0cm} L{3.4cm} L{3.6cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Case} & \textbf{L1 (CLI)} & \textbf{L2 (HTTP)} & \textbf{L3 (state)} \\
\midrule
\endfirsthead
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Case} & \textbf{L1} & \textbf{L2} & \textbf{L3} \\
\midrule
\endhead
M1 & \texttt{pages.publish} of two-file folder & exit 0; stdout has \texttt{rid} + \texttt{url} & --- & \texttt{PublishedResources} contains the rid; ability registry contains \texttt{pages.<rid>.fetch}; canonical receipt logged \\
M2 & \texttt{GET /hello-world.html} & --- & 200; \texttt{text/html}; body bytes match file & operational receipt logged \\
M3 & \texttt{GET /style.css} & --- & 200; \texttt{text/css}; body bytes match file & operational receipt logged \\
M4 & \texttt{GET /../../etc/passwd} (path traversal) & --- & 404 & daemon never opens the target file; \texttt{openat2} returns \texttt{EXDEV}; no read syscall to \texttt{/etc/passwd} \\
M5 & \texttt{GET /nonexistent.html} & --- & 404 & no successful read \\
M6 & dotfile probe (\texttt{GET /.env} or \texttt{/.git/HEAD}) & --- & 404 & blocked by default-deny dotfile rule \\
M7 & symlink escape (folder contains \texttt{escape \(\to\) /etc/passwd}) & --- & 404 & \texttt{RESOLVE\_NO\_SYMLINKS} blocks open \\
\bottomrule
\end{xltabular}

\noindent A phase ships only when \emph{every} case in the
matrix passes at all three layers. M4 + M6 + M7 are the security
gates and must be explicit, not implicit.

