% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Beyond MVP --- post-v0.5 work, in shape only}
\label{sec:post-mvp}

The following is intentionally not specified in v0.5 and is
listed only so that none of it leaks into the MVP code:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item \textbf{SPA support.} \texttt{spa\_fallback} arg on
    publish; fetch returns \texttt{index.html} on path miss.
    \item \textbf{API surface.} Additional resource-owned
    abilities under \texttt{ability/pages.<rid>.api.<verb>};
    Hub has a parallel route \texttt{/api/<verb>}.
    \item \textbf{Peer-agent action discovery.} Resource-owned
    \texttt{actions.*} abilities for runtime intent discovery
    by other agents.
    \item \textbf{Visibility = PRIVATE / SCOPED.} Capability-URL
    semantics; rid extends to full sha256.
    \item \textbf{Federation.} Content-addressed rid means a
    second host with the same bytes can serve the same URL;
    multi-host serve becomes natural.
    \item \textbf{User-friendly aliases.} \texttt{pages.alias}
    ability mapping a slug to a rid;
    \texttt{<slug>.<owner>.pages.<realm>} as URL.
    \item \textbf{Daemon-restart persistence.}
    \texttt{PublishedResources} survives restart by re-opening
    folder fds from a persisted manifest.
\end{itemize}

Each of these is purely additive. None requires changing the
shape established in §\ref{sec:primitive}: publish mints a
resource, the resource owns its read abilities, the Hub is the
HTTP boundary, and the read path is sandboxed.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Open decisions}
\label{sec:open}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{Hub binary placement (MVP vs production).} v0.5 lets the
daemon serve \texttt{*.pages.<realm>} on a local port for MVP.
Production needs the Hub to live in the backend (Go), where
TLS, wildcard certs, and shared cookie domain already exist.
The cut-over is mechanical: replace the daemon's \texttt{pages.serve}
with a Go ability that does the same \texttt{forward\_invoke}.
The choice does not affect ontology.
\end{decisionbox}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{macOS sandbox parity.} The macOS read sandbox is
weaker than Linux's \texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH} in v0.5. Two
strengthening paths exist: (a) per-publish sandboxed worker
process, profile-pinned to the published folder; (b) wait for
Apple's \texttt{O\_RESOLVE\_BENEATH} equivalent (rumoured,
unconfirmed). Production targets Linux; macOS parity is a
follow-up.
\end{decisionbox}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{rid length policy.} v0.5 uses 16 hex (64 bits) for
PUBLIC. PRIVATE/SCOPED, when implemented, will need 64 hex or
a separately-minted 256-bit token. The chosen length must be
recorded in the publish receipt so future verification cannot
silently widen attack surface.
\end{decisionbox}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Coverage check}

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X l}
\toprule
\textbf{Rule} & \textbf{Discharged by} & \textbf{Where} \\
\midrule
URA v4.1.5 §A.URA-7 (3-segment URA)        & Resource URA + ability URA each 3 segments  & \S\ref{sec:primitive} \\
URA v4.1.5 §9 (callee \(\in\) hub/device/agent) & Callee on fetch is \texttt{agent/<host>}    & \S\ref{sec:primitive} \\
ONTOLOGY §3.2 (skill = ability with visibility) & PRIVATE/SCOPED reuse the existing visibility enum & \S\ref{sec:post-mvp} \\
AXIOM (every invocation produces a receipt) & Two-tier classification (canonical / operational) & \S\ref{sec:receipts} \\
forward\_invoke endgame contract           & Hub uses the standard forward\_invoke; no parallel path & \S\ref{sec:hub} \\
Sandbox discipline (kernel-enforced)        & PG-INV-3 (Linux \texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH})  & \S\ref{sec:sandbox} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\paragraph{Closing.} The minimum viable surface for ``serve a
folder over the web from EasyNet'' is one ability call, one
resource URA, one Hub-side serve ability, and one daemon-side
sandboxed read ability. The MVP is exactly this much code. The
ontology is exactly the ontology v4.1.5 already gives. Every
larger goal --- SPA, API, peer actions, federation --- is an
additive composition over this same shape, not a change to it.

\vfill
\hrule
\vspace{0.4em}
\noindent\small
\textit{An ability rooted at a resource is not a new ontology
primitive. It is the ontology v4.1.5 already gives, applied to
a resource that has methods.}

